== One event, many dimensions — the demonetisation story

On 8 November 2016 India cancelled its ₹500 and ₹1000 currency notes
overnight. The syllabus's own running example, demonetisation shows how a
single event lands on *every* dimension at once — and questions love exactly
this crossing.

#table(
  columns: (1.05fr, 1.9fr),
  table.header[*Dimension*][*How demonetisation hit it*],
  [Economic], [Currency withdrawn overnight — the money supply itself changed],
  [Political], [A government move against black money and tax evasion — a political decision with an economic weapon],
  [Legal], [Carried out through an RBI notification under the RBI Act, 1934 — the legal route made it valid],
  [Technological], [A surge in digital payments — wallets and UPI apps grew within months],
  [Social], [People's payment habits changed — queues, then apps; society adjusted how it pays],
)

- *The lesson to carry:* when a question describes a big national event, do
  not force it into one dimension — first ask *which faces* of the event are
  being shown, then answer with as many dimensions as the wording demands.

= Impact of the Environment on Business

Beyond classification, the environment and business *transact* with each
other continuously — and the syllabus expects you to describe that exchange.

- *Environment supplies inputs:* men and women to hire, money to borrow,
  materials to process, power to run machines, information to decide with —
  the firm draws all of these from outside.
- *Business returns outputs:* goods and services for customers, wages for
  households, taxes for the government — the loop closes back into the same
  environment that fed it.
- *Inter-relatedness in action:* the forces move together. Soft drinks sell
  more  $arrow$  more glass, more refrigerators, more delivery vehicles are
  needed — a change in one industry's environment is another industry's
  opportunity.
- *The same environment, different readings:* a weak rupee terrifies an
  importer and delights an exporter. Environment is *relative* — the firm's
  own position decides whether a force is a threat or a gift.

