= Common Errors and Special Cases

#exception-box(title: "Common Errors & Special Cases")[
  #table(
    columns: (1.35fr, 1.35fr, 1.2fr),
    table.header[*The mistake students make*][*What is right*][*The why / the trap*],
    ["Political and legal dimensions are the same"], [Political = *stability and attitude* of government; legal = *actual laws, orders, judgments*], [The mood is political; the rulebook is legal — a decision announced is political, the notification that enforces it is legal.],
    ["Environment includes forces inside the firm"], [Environment is *external only* — outside the firm's control], [Employees and machines belong to the firm; interest rates and laws do not.],
    ["GST belongs to only one dimension"], [Big reforms span *several* dimensions at once], [GST is economic (tax), legal (the GST Act) and political (a policy choice) — pick what the question describes.],
    ["First mover advantage is an example of tapping resources"], [It illustrates *identifying opportunities*], [Tapping resources is about inputs; first mover is about spotting demand before rivals.],
    ["A stable environment needs no scanning"], [Environment is *dynamic and uncertain* — scanning never stops], [Fashion and technology can overturn a market within months; yesterday's scan is stale.],
    ["Uncertainty and complexity are the same feature"], [Uncertain = *hard to predict*; complex = *many forces, hard to grasp together*], [One is about the future's unpredictability, the other about today's tangle.],
  )
]

- *Spot the pattern:* dimension questions are *sorting* questions — read the
  event, name the shelf it sits on, and check whether the wording names more
  than one shelf.
- *One special case:* if an event description mentions both a *government
  decision* and the *notification or act* that enforced it, the intended
  answer is usually *political and legal together*.

