= Practice Questions

#mcq(1, [The demand for soft drinks rises, and with it the demand for glass bottles and refrigerators rises too. Which feature of the business environment does this illustrate?], (
  [uncertainty], [relativity], [inter-relatedness], [complexity],
))

#mcq(2, [A firm that spots a new consumer need before its competitors enter the market gains its profit from being first. This importance of understanding the environment is called:], (
  [tapping useful resources], [first mover advantage], [early warning signal], [planning and policy formulation],
))

#mcq(3, [Compulsory statutory warnings printed on tobacco packaging illustrate which dimension of the business environment?], (
  [political], [legal], [social], [economic],
))

#mcq(4, [The attitude of a newly elected government towards foreign investment belongs to which dimension?], (
  [legal], [economic], [political], [technological],
))

#mcq(5, [An increase in the literacy rate of a region enlarges the market for books and e-learning. This change belongs to the:], (
  [social dimension], [economic dimension], [technological dimension], [political dimension],
))

#mcq(6, [A weak rupee worries an importing firm but delights an exporting firm in the same industry. This shows the environment is:], (
  [complex], [relative — the same force reads differently to different firms], [general and not specific], [certain and stable],
))

#mcq(7, [Which pair of forces is correctly classified as specific forces of the business environment?], (
  [government policy and social trends], [customers, suppliers and competitors], [inflation and interest rates], [court judgments and acts of Parliament],
))

#mcq(8, [Demonetisation in November 2016 is a favourite example because it touched:], (
  [only the economic dimension], [only the political and legal dimensions], [all five dimensions — economic, political, legal, technological and social], [only the technological dimension through digital payments],
))

#mcq(9, [The business environment supplies inputs to the firm. Which set names environment-supplied inputs correctly?], (
  [goods, services and taxes], [men, money, materials, power and information], [customers, brand value and employees' loyalty], [profit, dividend and reserves],
))

#mcq(10, ["Each force of the environment is easy to understand alone, but together they are hard to grasp." This describes the feature of:], (
  [complexity], [uncertainty], [dynamism], [relativity],
))

= Solutions

#solution(1, [Forces in the environment are *inter-related* — a change in one industry's demand travels to other industries. Soft-drink demand pulled glass and refrigerator demand along with it; that chain effect is the definition of inter-relatedness.], answer: "C")

#solution(2, [Spotting a need *before* competitors do — and profiting from entering first — is the *first mover advantage*: the first importance point of environment scanning. Tapping resources is about inputs; early warning is about threats.], answer: "B")

#solution(3, [A compulsory warning is enforced through law — legislation and orders make it binding. It is a *legal* dimension example; the government's attitude would have made it political, but the enforced rule is legal.], answer: "B")

#solution(4, [The government's *attitude* and stance towards business — before any law is framed — belongs to the *political* dimension. Once the stance becomes an act or notification, it turns legal.], answer: "C")

#solution(5, [Literacy, education and demographic change are features of how society lives — the *social dimension*. Rising literacy enlarging the book market is the standard social-dimension example.], answer: "A")

#solution(6, [The *same* environmental force — a weak rupee — is a threat to the importer and an opportunity to the exporter. Reading the same environment differently from different positions is *relativity*.], answer: "B")

#solution(7, [*Specific* forces affect one business or industry directly — customers, suppliers, competitors. Government policy, social trends, inflation and court judgments hit *all* firms — they are general forces.], answer: "B")

#solution(8, [Demonetisation was economic (currency withdrawn), political (a move against black money), legal (RBI notification under the RBI Act, 1934), technological (digital payments surged) and social (payment habits changed). It touched *all five dimensions*.], answer: "C")

#solution(9, [The environment *supplies* inputs — people, money, materials, power, information — and the firm *returns* outputs: goods, services and taxes. Option A lists outputs; the others are internal or financial results.], answer: "B")

#solution(10, [Complexity is about *many forces interacting* — each simple, the whole hard to grasp. Uncertainty is about the future being unpredictable; dynamism about constant change; relativity about different readings by different firms.], answer: "A")

